            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The sun primarily symbolizes God and especially Jesus Christ (the 'Sun of Righteousness'), with secondary meanings including earthly rulers or patriarchs, eschatological judgment, and the glorified righteous.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The sun symbolizes the Father (as the ultimate source of light) and the righteous who will shine like the sun in His kingdom.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"God, Jesus Christ, governing authorities/patriarchs, and the righteous.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The Father, and the righteous.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book narrows the divine meaning from 'God/Christ' generally to 'The Father' specifically. This elegantly unites the divine symbol (Ps 84:11) with the earthly narrative symbol in Genesis 37:9 (where the sun represents Jacob, the father) and Matthew 13:43 (the kingdom of their Father), but it leaves out the highly prominent Christological application of Malachi 4:2.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. Naming the Father as the ultimate source of light does not necessarily deny that Christ is the 'Sun of Righteousness,' though the book restricts its entry's focus to the paternal aspect.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book relies on accepted consensus texts (Ps 84:11, Gen 37:9, Matt 13:43) but refines the overarching concept to 'The Father.' This synthesizes the heavenly and earthly references under a single, tighter thematic category while shedding the consensus's heavy emphasis on Christological readings.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"By focusing exclusively on the Father, the book ignores Malachi 4:2, which has historically been the primary anchor for interpreting the sun as a symbol for Christ.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
